# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
# System accounts for CARLOS EMR.
#
# Two accounts, deliberately separate, and neither is a member of any
# privileged group:
#
#   carlos         runs the JVM. Owns the document store and Tomcat's writable
#                  state. Holds the read-write database credential.
#   carlos-backup  runs the backup and restore-drill timers. Holds a
#                  read-only database credential and the restic passphrase,
#                  and owns the backup repository — none of which the `carlos`
#                  uid can read.
#   carlos-render  runs the headless browser that renders eForms to PDF, and is
#                  a member of NO group — deliberately, including not `carlos`.
#                  DECLARED IN carlos-emr-eform-renderer.sysusers, not here: the
#                  account and the directory it owns must be created by the same
#                  package, or carlos-emr's tmpfiles run first on a clean install
#                  and fail with "Failed to resolve user 'carlos-render'".
#
# Why carlos-render exists at all: chromedriver listens on a loopback TCP port
# with no authentication, and a WebDriver new-session request can specify the
# browser binary and its arguments. That makes an exposed chromedriver an
# arbitrary-code-execution service running as whatever account it was started
# with. Started as `carlos` it would hand an attacker the uid that owns
# /var/lib/carlos-emr/CarlosDocument (scanned patient records) and can read
# carlos.properties (database credentials, the stored-credential key). Started as
# carlos-render it yields a browser owning nothing but a scratch home.
#
# This is only possible because the renderer needs no shared filesystem with the
# application: the rendered PDF comes back inline over the DevTools protocol, not
# through a file both sides can see.
#
# The account split is the ONLY real control here, which is why it is not
# optional. The --url-base token is a speed bump, not a boundary: systemd expands
# it into the process's argv and /proc/<pid>/cmdline is world-readable, so any
# local uid can read it. Do not reason as though it protects the port.
#
# And be precise about what carlos-render does reach: it is outside the EMR's
# data, but it is inside the PHI boundary of the form being rendered, and it can
# talk to Tomcat, DrugRef and MariaDB on loopback — i.e. the application's own
# HTTP surface with nginx and ModSecurity bypassed. Compromising it is not
# nothing; it is bounded.
#
# The relationship between them is deliberately ONE-WAY. carlos-backup is a
# member of the `carlos` group, so it can READ everything the application owns
# — the document store, carlos.properties, the encryption key the application
# uses for stored credentials — which is exactly what a restorable backup
# needs. `carlos` is NOT a member of any group of carlos-backup's, so the
# application cannot read the repository, the passphrase or the backup
# credential. A compromise of the EMR therefore cannot quietly destroy,
# decrypt or exfiltrate the backups that would be used to recover from it.
#
# The application is NOT run under the distribution's shared `tomcat` user.
# That account is shared with every other Tomcat webapp on the machine, and on
# a host running an EMR that is precisely the boundary that has to hold.
u carlos       - "CARLOS EMR application server" /var/lib/carlos-emr        /usr/sbin/nologin
u carlos-backup - "CARLOS EMR backup" /var/backups/carlos-emr /usr/sbin/nologin
m carlos-backup carlos
